o \"aceg\"). After that the chosen string t is appended to the end of s.Mister B soon found the game boring and came up with the following question: what can be the minimum possible number of different letters in string s on the segment between positions l and r, inclusive. Letters of string s are numerated starting from 1.\n</problem_description>\n\nHere is the Java 8 code you have to repair:\n<original_code>\nimport java.io.*;\nimport java.util.*;\n\npublic class Div2_421C {\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {\n        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);\n        int a = sc.nextInt();\n        int b = sc.nextInt();\n        int l = sc.nextInt();\n        int r = sc.nextInt();\n        sc.close();\n\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\n        for (int i = 0; i < a; i++) {\n            sb.append((char) ('a' + i));\n        }\n        char last = sb.charAt(sb.length() - 1);\n        sb.append(String.valueOf(last).repeat(Math.max(0, b)));\n\n        Set<Integer> used = new TreeSet<>();\n        int start = sb.length() - a;\n        for (int i = start; i < sb.length(); i++) {\n            used.add(sb.charAt(i) - 'a');\n        }\n\n        int next = 0;\n        for (int i = 0; i < a; i++) {\n            while (used.contains(next)) next++;\n            sb.append((char) ('a' + next));\n            next++;\n        }\n\n        last = sb.charAt(sb.length() - 1);\n        sb.append(String.valueOf(last).repeat(Math.max(0, b)));\n\n        int len = sb.length();\n        used.clear();\n\n        if (r - l + 1 >= len) {\n            for (char c : sb.toString().toCharArray()) {\n                used.add(c - 'a');\n            }\n        } else {\n            for (int i = l - 1; i < r; i++) {\n                used.add(sb.charAt(i % len) - 'a');\n            }\n        }\n\n        System.out.println(used.size());\n    }\n}\n</original_code>\n"}], "temp": 0, "sample_size": 1, "logprobs": true, "model": "qwen/qwen3-coder-next"}, "response": {"samples": [{"content": "<repaired_code>\nimport java.io.*;\nimport java.util.*;\n\npublic class Div2_421C {\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {\n        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);\n        int a = sc.nextInt();\n        int b = sc.nextInt();\n        int l = sc.nextInt();\n        int r = sc.nextInt();\n        sc.close();\n\n        // Build initial string: first a lowercase letters\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\n        for (int i = 0; i < a; i++) {\n            sb.append((char) ('a' + i));\n        }\n\n        // Simulate the game until we cover position r\n        while (sb.length() < r) {\n            // Mister B's turn: append b letters (he can choose optimally to minimize distinct letters)\n            // To minimize distinct letters in the final segment, Mister B should append the same letter\n            // But we need to consider the optimal strategy for minimizing the segment [l, r]\n            // Actually, Mister B wants to minimize the number of distinct letters in [l, r]\n            // So he should append letters that help minimize distinct count in that range\n            \n            // However, the problem asks for the minimum possible number of different letters\n            // So we need to simulate with optimal play from Mister B\n            \n            // For Mister B's move: he can choose any b letters. To minimize distinct letters\n            // in the target segment, he should choose letters that are already present or\n            // will help create a pattern with fewer distinct letters\n            \n            // But the key insight: Mister B can choose letters arbitrarily, so he can choose\n            // to repeat the last letter of current string b times to minimize new distinct letters\n            \n            char lastChar = sb.charAt(sb.length() - 1);\n            for (int i = 0; i < b; i++) {\n                sb.append(lastChar);\n            }\n            \n            // Computer's turn: append a letters based on the last a characters\n            String suffix = sb.substring(sb.length() - a);\n            Set<Integer> usedInSuffix = new HashSet<>();\n            for (char c : suffix.toCharArray()) {\n                usedInSuffix.add(c - 'a');\n            }\n            \n            // Find lexicographically smallest string with distinct letters not in suffix\n            StringBuilder compMove = new StringBuilder();\n            int nextChar = 0;\n            for (int i = 0; i < a; i++) {\n                while (usedInSuffix.contains(nextChar)) {\n                    nextChar++;\n                }\n                compMove.append((char) ('a' + nextChar));\n                nextChar++;\n            }\n            sb.append(compMove.toString());\n        }\n\n        // Count distinct letters in segment [l, r] (1-indexed)\n        Set<Integer> distinct = new HashSet<>();\n        for (int i = l - 1; i < r; i++) {\n            distinct.add(sb.charAt(i) - 'a');\n        }\n        \n        System.out.println(distinct.size());\n    }\n}\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n0.3\n</confidence>", "token_logprobs": [-4.768370445162873e-07, -0.00011622230522334576, -3.814689989667386e-06, -4.768370445162873e-07, -0.000164018536452204, -1.5139465176616795e-05, -0.00015841660206206143, -5.483612312673358e-06, -3.707340147229843e-05, -8.34461570775602e-06, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -2.7656173188006505e-05, -7.629365427419543e-06, -5.960462772236497e-07, -7.807903602952138e-05, -1.311301275563892e-06, -5.960462772236497e-07, -5.960462772236497e-07, -1.156323378381785e-05, -5.960462772236497e-07, -1.0728830375228426e-06, -0.0004314445541240275, -5.960462772236497e-07, -1.2278481335670222e-05, -1.0728830375228426e-06, -1.7881377516459906e-06, -1.0371154530730564e-05, -1.5497195136049413e-06, -6.23445157543756e-05, -8.821448318485636e-06, -1.5616295058862306e-05, -0.00020716428116429597, -2.5629668016335927e-05, -3.5762781180892